 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to liability for the\npayment of fees for services rendered by the department of mental hygiene",
    "basePrintNo": "S4255",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4255-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §43.03, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4255\n\nSPONSOR: CARLUCCI\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\nliability for the payment of fees for services rendered by the depart-\nment of mental hygiene\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would prohibit OMH from billing a person for his/her care and\ntreatment when the source of the funds for making such a payment comes\nfrom the proceeds of a suit against the State for negligent or improper\ntreatment.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill adds a new subdivision (e) to section 43.03 of the mental\nhygiene law which specifically adds language to prevent monetary awards\narising from judgments or settlements as a result of a cause of action\nagainst state employees or officials to be used to pay for their care\nand treatment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill was introduced in response to the case of a Mr. Kaplan who was\nstabbed to death while he was a patient at Kingsboro PC. The family sued\nthe State and then received a bill for the care and treatment of this\nman, including the day that he was murdered by his roommate. There have\nbeen other similar cases, including two rape cases and an attempted\nsuicide. This practice, which has not been uncommon, has a chilling\neffect on a patient's right to sue the State for redress for serious\ninjury or death. It removes the incentive for the State to correct\nunsafe or abusive practices.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2017-18: A3134 Cal. 254/S2341 referred to Mental Health\n2015-16: A3854 advanced to third reading/S1754 referred to Mental Health\n2013-14: A5153 passed Assembly/S7589 referred to Mental Health\n2011-12: A4346A passed Assembly\n2009-10: A2526 referred to Mental Health\n2007-08: A1438 passed Assembly\n2005-06: A2019 passed Assembly\n2003-04: A2713 passed Assembly\n2001-02: A2619 passed Assembly/S1637 referred to Mental Health\n1999-2000: A3639B passed Assembly/S4839A referred to Mental Health\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately\n",
